    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm reviewing a commercial lease for a retail tenant in Singapore. Can you explain the standard legal implications of a \"force majeure\" clause in the event of a prolonged, unforeseen business interruption that prevents the tenant from operating?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A \"force majeure\" clause typically frees both parties from liability or obligation when an extraordinary event or circumstance beyond their control occurs and prevents one or both of the parties from fulfilling their contractual duties. In the context of a prolonged business interruption, if the lease includes such a clause and the event is covered by it, the tenant (retailer) may not be held responsible for failing to meet its obligations, such as paying rent. It may allow for renegotiation of lease terms or provide exemptions from penalties. However, the specific terms of the clause, including what events are covered and the tenant's obligations upon such an event, should be carefully reviewed. Not all interruptions fall under \"force majeure,\" so this clause needs to be scrutinized in relation to the situation.",
      "source": "target",
