    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Does the clause typically cover government-mandated shutdowns like we saw during the pandemic, or is it strictly for natural disasters? Also, how is \"unforeseeability\" usually interpreted by courts in these cases?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Force majeure clauses can indeed cover government-mandated shutdowns, as governments' actions can be seen as an unforeseen circumstance beyond the control of contracting parties. However, the inclusion, interpretation, and applicability depend on the specific wording of the clause. \"Unforeseeability\" in the context of force majeure is generally assessed on whether the event could not have been reasonably anticipated at the time of the contract signing. Courts look for evidence that both parties lacked the capacity to foresee the event at that time. During the pandemic, for instance, while it could be argued reasonably that a global pandemic of this severity was unforeseeable, there might still be a legal debate regarding whether such an event was truly beyond the parties' contemplation. The interpretation of unforeseeability can vary depending on the jurisdiction's legal standards and the specific facts of the case. Past jurisprudence may offer insight into what courts in those jurisdictions typically look for when evaluating such clauses.",
      "source": "target",
