    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Understood. Following up on that, if a tenant tries to invoke force majeure due to some kind of sudden, chaotic disruption that wasn't explicitly listed in the clause—say, some unexpected civil unrest or a massive supply chain cascade—how much weight does the specific \"list\" of events in the contract actually carry versus the general principle of events being \"beyond control\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The specific \"list\" of events in a force majeure clause is crucial since it outlines what the parties have agreed to be covered by the clause. If an event closely matches an item on the list, invoking force majeure could be clearer and more straightforward. However, if the event wasn't explicitly listed, courts will typically look to the clause's general principle of covering events beyond the control of the parties. In such instances, they will interpret the clause to determine if the unexpected event falls within the spirit and scope of the clause. Consistency in the contractual language makes the list compelling, while the general principle gives courts some discretion in unforeseen circumstances. The specific event's relationship to the list of triggering events, the contract's overall intent, and how similar the event is to those listed, will weigh heavily in the court's interpretation.",
      "source": "target",
